WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:03.567
This is Greenroom, the open-source speaker
and session management software for

00:03.667 --> 00:05.167
conferences. Here's a demo.

00:05.267 --> 00:09.367
We're starting on the Greenroom home
screen for AI Engineer Summit 2026.

00:09.467 --> 00:13.429
I'm signed in as an organizer so we can
follow one proposal all the way from the

00:13.529 --> 00:15.696
call for speakers to the public schedule.

00:15.729 --> 00:19.296
First, I'm opening forms and
selecting the main call for speakers.

00:19.329 --> 00:22.883
This screen contains the public welcome
message, the questions speakers will

00:22.983 --> 00:27.017
answer, the confirmation email, and the
dates when the form opens and closes.

00:27.050 --> 00:29.417
Here's a question called
workshop requirements.

00:29.450 --> 00:32.950
It has a conditional badge, and when
I open it, you can see the rule.

00:33.050 --> 00:36.879
Only show this question when session
format is set to a 90-minute workshop.

00:36.913 --> 00:40.146
The form also allows Co-speakers
That switch is already enabled,

00:40.246 --> 00:43.563
so a person submitting a talk can add
someone else to the same proposal.

00:43.596 --> 00:47.029
Both people will stay connected to the
submission as it moves through the event.

00:47.225 --> 00:49.592
Farther down, we can see
the confirmation email.

00:49.692 --> 00:53.058
The organizer controls the subject and
message, and the preview on the bottom

00:53.158 --> 00:56.725
shows how the merge fields will look
when real proposal details are inserted.

00:56.758 --> 01:00.208
At the bottom are the public URL and
the form's opening and closing dates.

01:00.338 --> 01:04.038
Everything looks ready so I'm saving the
form and then opening its public link.

01:04.071 --> 01:07.304
Now we're looking at the speaker-facing
form with no organizer session.

01:07.404 --> 01:10.038
The welcome copy appears at
the top followed by the title,

01:10.138 --> 01:13.425
abstract, speaker details, and
the organizer's custom questions.

01:13.458 --> 01:17.275
I'll enter the proposal title and abstract
then move through the remaining fields.

01:17.308 --> 01:21.075
For the track, I'm choosing Evals &
Reliability which will later help route

01:21.175 --> 01:22.688
this proposal to the right reviewers.

01:22.721 --> 01:24.688
Watch the session format field.

01:24.721 --> 01:28.354
When I choose 90-minute workshop, the
workshop requirements question appears

01:28.454 --> 01:31.354
immediately. When I switch
back to a 45-minute talk,

01:31.454 --> 01:34.704
that extra question disappears because
its condition no longer matches.

01:37.467 --> 01:41.433
Co-speakers are enabled, so I'm
adding Owen Diallo alongside Nadia.

01:41.533 --> 01:44.500
I'll fill in Owen's information,
finish the required answers,

01:44.600 --> 01:45.833
and submit the proposal.

01:45.867 --> 01:49.813
The confirmation page shows that the
proposal was received and that an email is

01:49.913 --> 01:52.613
being sent. Let's switch
back to the organizer view.

01:52.713 --> 01:56.146
On the submission screen, I'm
filtering the queue to unreviewed.

01:56.246 --> 02:00.692
The new proposal is at the top with Nadia
and Owen listed together and its Evals &

02:00.792 --> 02:03.175
Reliability track is visible in the row.

02:03.208 --> 02:07.025
Opening the proposal shows the full
abstract and every submitted answer.

02:07.058 --> 02:10.025
The co speaker section
includes Owen's name, role,

02:10.125 --> 02:13.646
and company so the organizer has
the complete proposal in one place.

02:13.746 --> 02:17.646
The abstract needs a small revision, so
I'm choosing request changes instead of

02:17.746 --> 02:21.646
recording a decision. In the dialog I'm
writing a note about what to update and

02:21.746 --> 02:22.596
setting a deadline.

02:24.450 --> 02:26.550
The proposal stays unreviewed.

02:26.650 --> 02:30.483
Next, I'm opening review rounds and going
into the First-pass review assignments.

02:30.517 --> 02:35.017
I'm assigning this proposal to Dana, one
of the reviewers for Evals & Reliability.

02:35.050 --> 02:37.446
Now I'll switch into
Dana's reviewer account.

02:38.129 --> 02:41.429
On Dana's assigned proposal, I'm
opening the First-pass scorecard.

02:41.529 --> 02:44.763
The title and proposal content are
visible, but Nadia and Owen's identities

02:44.863 --> 02:47.163
are not shown because this round is blind.

02:47.263 --> 02:50.475
The scorecard has two numeric
ratings, a recommendation field,

02:50.575 --> 02:54.208
and a comments box. I'll enter Dana's
ratings, choose accept as her round

02:54.308 --> 02:56.958
recommendation, add her
reasoning, and submit.

02:57.042 --> 02:58.958
I'm switching back to
the organizer account.

02:58.996 --> 03:03.096
On the proposal, Dana's submitted ratings
recommendation and written comments are

03:03.196 --> 03:04.763
all visible in the review section.

03:04.796 --> 03:06.963
The organizer is ready
to accept the proposal.

03:07.063 --> 03:10.763
I'm choosing accept, adding a note for
Nadia and Owen and leaving the email

03:10.863 --> 03:15.092
option enabled. The dialog explains that
this will create a session and assign the

03:15.192 --> 03:16.708
event’s onboarding tasks.

03:16.858 --> 03:20.225
Back on the proposal record, the
decision shows accepted by Avery Chen.

03:20.325 --> 03:23.729
The note to the speakers appears below it,
along with links to the new session and

03:23.829 --> 03:25.796
the onboarding work
created by the decision.

03:25.829 --> 03:27.796
Now we'll sign in as Nadia.

03:27.829 --> 03:30.729
Nadia's portal shows the approved
proposal, the new session,

03:30.829 --> 03:32.396
and its current unscheduled state.

03:32.429 --> 03:35.633
Below that are the six onboarding
tasks assigned to her when the talk was

03:35.733 --> 03:36.383
accepted.

03:38.933 --> 03:42.500
Switching back to the organizer, the
Speakers screen reflects Nadia's progress

03:42.600 --> 03:45.650
immediately. Her row shows
the updated completion count,

03:45.683 --> 03:49.154
overdue work, and an individual
indicator for each task.

03:49.438 --> 03:50.938
Next is the agenda board.

03:50.971 --> 03:54.404
The unscheduled tray contains the
new session, and the schedule grid is

03:54.504 --> 03:56.833
organized by day, room, and time.

03:56.867 --> 04:01.017
I'm deliberately dragging the new session
onto Main Stage at 10:00 where another

04:01.117 --> 04:05.283
session already exists. The placement is
saved and both cards turn red to show the

04:05.383 --> 04:09.167
room conflict. I'll fix it by moving
the new session to Workshop B at 1:00.

04:09.296 --> 04:12.329
The red outlines clear and the
conflict count returns to zero.

04:12.363 --> 04:13.863
Now I’m opening Communications.

04:13.963 --> 04:17.129
the log already contains the proposal
confirmation, the change request,

04:17.229 --> 04:18.396
and the acceptance messages.

04:18.496 --> 04:22.217
The task digest action sends each eligible
speaker one message containing all of

04:22.317 --> 04:26.017
their open tasks. The digest button is
disabled because the recipients were

04:26.117 --> 04:29.950
contacted recently. The explanation
underneath shows that the 24-hour guard is

04:30.050 --> 04:31.500
preventing another immediate reminder.

04:32.013 --> 04:34.913
In templates, the event has
a set of built-in messages.

04:34.946 --> 04:38.246
I'm opening one and adding a merge
field that this message cannot supply.

04:38.279 --> 04:40.779
Validation catches it
and keeps save disabled.

04:40.813 --> 04:43.196
I'll replace that value with a
supported field and save again.

04:43.350 --> 04:45.250
Next is the calendar invitation screen.

04:45.350 --> 04:48.517
The new session row shows
Workshop B, its scheduled time,

04:48.617 --> 04:50.783
and both Nadia and Owen as recipients.

04:50.817 --> 04:53.000
I'm sending the invitation
to both speakers.

04:53.350 --> 04:55.717
Finally, we're opening
the public event page.

04:55.817 --> 04:59.338
It shows the event dates, venue,
navigation, and links to the currently

04:59.438 --> 05:00.804
open calls for speakers.

05:00.838 --> 05:05.138
On the public schedule, the newly accepted
session is now listed on day one at 1:00

05:05.238 --> 05:08.288
in Workshop B, with Nadia and
Owen shown as its speakers.

05:08.567 --> 05:11.267
The same people also appear in
the public speaker directory.

05:11.367 --> 05:14.467
The Embeds screen offers the
schedule as a script, an iframe,

05:14.567 --> 05:16.950
JSON, XML, and iCalendar.

05:17.300 --> 05:18.400
That completes the flow.

05:18.500 --> 05:21.546
We created a proposal with a
co-speaker, requested a revision,

05:21.646 --> 05:24.413
assigned and filed a blind
scorecard, accepted the talk,

05:24.513 --> 05:27.546
completed speaker onboarding
work, resolved an agenda conflict,

05:27.646 --> 05:31.079
sent communications and calendar updates,
and confirmed the session on the public

05:31.179 --> 05:32.400
program. Thanks for watching.
